Assuming reset is asserted at least once, these lemmas cover both the default `WIDTH=8` and narrower widths. The blocking assignment increments `i` before the `% 7` test; concurrent assertions observe the resulting state at the next sampled clock edge. ([accellera.org](https://accellera.org/images/eda/sv-ec/att-1279/01-Scheduling_clarification_questions_20030720.PDF?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (i <= N); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Bounds the loop index. For widths that can represent 56, this rules out an overshoot and establishes that the transition to DONE occurs at i == N."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) ((WIDTH < 6) || (sum == i / 7)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "For WIDTH >= 6, sum counts the multiples of seven reached by i, including 56. The width guard is necessary because narrower counters wrap before reaching N."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) ((state != DONE) || ((WIDTH >= 6) && (i == N))); endproperty",
      "explanation": "DONE is reachable only after i reaches 56; with WIDTH < 6, it is unreachable. Together with lemma_2, DONE implies sum == 56 / 7 == 8."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The key invariant is that `sum` counts multiples of 7 reached by `i`. Because `i = i + 1` is a blocking assignment, the `% 7` test uses the **incremented** value. The lemmas below are intended to be proved together, assuming reset is asserted to establish the initial state. ([rfsoc.mit.edu](https://rfsoc.mit.edu/6S965/_static/F24/documentation/1800-2017.pdf?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (i <= N); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Bounds the index at 56: LOOP increments only while i is below N, and otherwise stops incrementing. This makes the transition condition i >= N equivalent to i == N."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) ((WIDTH < 6) || (sum == (i / 7))); endproperty",
      "explanation": "For widths that can represent 56, each increment to a multiple of 7 increments sum, so sum tracks the integer quotient i/7. The width guard is necessary because narrower counters wrap."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) ((state != DONE) || (i == N)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "DONE is entered only when LOOP observes i >= N; lemma_1 reduces that condition to i == N. For WIDTH < 6, i cannot reach N, so DONE is unreachable."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

For `WIDTH >= 6`, lemmas 2 and 3 give `sum == 56/7 == 8` in `DONE`. For narrower widths, lemma 3 makes the target property vacuous.

The key is that the blocking assignment increments `i` **before** the `% 7` test. Thus, for the default `WIDTH=8`, `sum` tracks `i/7`; the transition to `DONE` occurs one clock after `i` reaches 56. The lemmas below also cover narrower positive widths, where `i` cannot reach 56 and `DONE` is unreachable. They assume formal verification begins with reset applied. ([rfsoc.mit.edu](https://rfsoc.mit.edu/6S965/_static/F25/documentation/1800-2017.pdf?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (WIDTH >= 6) |-> (i <= N); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Bounds the index at 56 for widths that can represent it, preventing wraparound in the counting invariant."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (WIDTH >= 6) |-> (sum == i / 7); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Relates the count to the incremented index: sum increases exactly when the new i is a multiple of seven."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) ((WIDTH >= 6) && (state == DONE)) |-> (i == N); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The index bound means LOOP can enter DONE only at i=56; lemma_2 then gives sum=56/7=8."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_4; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (WIDTH < 6) |-> (state == LOOP); endproperty",
      "explanation": "For positive widths below six, i is always less than 56, so the transition to DONE cannot occur."
    }
  ]
}
</json>
